ROAST.PET WEIRD BUILDER PROJECTS =E2=80=94 2026-09-20 06:23 Asia/Phnom_Penh

This pass screened 25+ plausible projects/results across GitHub and
adjacent web results for pet generation, POD/Etsy automation, caption
rendering, animal CV, WebAR, transparent video, matting, pose and
identity consistency. Prior-run finds were deduplicated. Three new
projects are strong enough to retain rather than padding the report.

1) PRESSWORK =E2=80=94 https://github.com/brac/presswork
Creator: brac
Activity: surfaced current, crawled 3 days ago
License: verify repository license before copying code
Verdict: COPY MECHANISM / BENCHMARK

What it solves: the boring autonomous commerce state machine after
Roast.pet has generated a finished product asset. Presswork is a
four-agent Etsy/POD pipeline: Scout =E2=86=92 Design =E2=86=92 Listing =E2=
=86=92 Ledger,
coordinated through Supabase. The useful part for Roast.pet is not its
trend scouting or generic art generation; it is the job handoff/state
model. Status columns enforce pending =E2=86=92 processing =E2=86=92 done/e=
rror, while
SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED prevents two workers claiming the
same job. It separates design_packages, listings and orders, and
leaves physical fulfillment to Etsy's native Printify integration.

Install/route: Supabase + separate Railway services/cron workers. Etsy
live credentials are optional; it runs against mocks until enabled.
Dependencies/hardware: ordinary cloud services; no GPU requirement for
orchestration.
Evidence: concrete schema, worker schedules, locking strategy,
human-review gate and explicit POD economics are documented.
Code/work replaced: potentially most of the order/listing/fulfillment
orchestration and concurrency logic we would otherwise write around
Etsy/Printify.
Integration difficulty: MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM for the state-machine ideas; Etsy API
remains upstream risk.
Exact experiment: model one fake Roast.pet order as immutable stages:
intake_approved =E2=86=92 pet_package_ready =E2=86=92 roast_video_ready =E2=
=86=92
print_assets_ready =E2=86=92 listing_ready =E2=86=92 printify_ready =E2=86=
=92 fulfilled. Run
two workers concurrently against 100 fake orders, intentionally crash
workers mid-stage, and prove no duplicate listings/products are
created. Keep HUMAN_REVIEW_ENABLED=3Dtrue.

Why it matters: this reinforces a strong architectural rule. Roast.pet
should not be one giant workflow. Each expensive artifact should be a
durable package handed to the next idempotent worker. Failures should
resume at the failed stage, never restart the whole order.

2) AI PET PORTRAIT =E2=80=94 https://github.com/SamurAIGPT/ai-pet-portrait
Creator: SamurAIGPT
Activity: current enough to expose nano-banana-2-edit/MuAPI workflow;
crawled last month
License: MIT
Verdict: COPY/FORK CUSTOMER + ASYNC GENERATION SHELL

What it solves: much of the generic pet-personalization SaaS plumbing
surrounding generation. It already implements up-to-five pet reference
uploads, asynchronous image generation, webhook-backed result
delivery, persisted creations/gallery, mobile UI, authentication,
credits and Stripe billing.

The most relevant mechanism is the async generation contract: browser
uploads references =E2=86=92 generation request is persisted =E2=86=92 exte=
rnal image
provider works asynchronously =E2=86=92 webhook writes completed output int=
o
Postgres =E2=86=92 gallery reflects result. That is extremely close to
Roast.pet's customer-facing generation shell, except our output is a
verified pet package + roast rather than a portrait.

Install/route: Next.js 14, Prisma/Postgres, NextAuth, Stripe, MuAPI;
deployable on Vercel. Local setup is npm install + Prisma database +
provider keys.
Dependencies/hardware: no local GPU; external generation API.
Evidence: live demo, explicit webhook endpoint, persisted gallery,
multi-image upload and complete documented app structure.
Code/work replaced: potentially the first version of customer
upload/history/status/result plumbing, auth, webhook delivery and
gallery UI.
Integration difficulty: MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because its exact provider stack is
replaceable and should not become core.
Exact experiment: fork only the upload =E2=86=92 job =E2=86=92 webhook =E2=
=86=92 gallery path.
Replace MuAPI-specific job payload with Roast.pet's provider-neutral
generation_job record. Submit five reference photos and return a fake
pet-package preview through the webhook. If this works cleanly, do not
build the customer async job shell from scratch.

Important: do not inherit its credit/billing assumptions blindly. The
reusable part is the async pet-generation product shell, not its
business model or provider choice.

3) SUBIT =E2=80=94 https://github.com/fariraimasocha/subit
Creator: fariraimasocha
Activity: surfaced current, crawled last month
License: verify before source reuse
Verdict: COPY CAPTION PREVIEW/EXPORT CONTRACT

What it solves: caption editing where browser preview and final FFmpeg
output agree. Subit transcribes video, exposes editable word-level
captions and styles, then burns them into MP4. The unusually useful
design choice is that preview and export share the same positioning
formula, reducing the classic 'looks right in browser, shifts in
render' problem.

Install/API/demo route: web dashboard + Whisper transcription + FFmpeg
render; uploaded bytes can go directly browser=E2=86=92R2 rather than throu=
gh
the app server.
Dependencies/hardware: Whisper path + FFmpeg; ordinary browser frontend.
Evidence: repository documents the project lifecycle and explicitly
states preview/export agreement by construction.
Code/work replaced: our custom caption positioning/editor math and
potentially direct-upload plumbing.
Integration difficulty: LOW-MEDIUM.
Maintenance risk: LOW for the mechanism.
Exact experiment: represent one Roast.pet vertical caption style using
the same positioning function in browser preview and ASS/FFmpeg
export. Render 20 lines at 1080=C3=971920, including very long/short words,
and pixel-compare caption bounding boxes. If deterministic, make this
the rule for all Roast.pet text overlays.

This is complementary to HeadTTS from the prior run: HeadTTS should
still supply known word timing for generated speech. Subit is useful
for how those timings become editable, predictable rendered
captions=E2=80=94not for retranscribing our own TTS.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

1. A home-grown distributed order state machine. Copy Presswork's
durable stage/status + row-locking pattern.
2. Generic customer upload =E2=86=92 async generation =E2=86=92 webhook =E2=
=86=92 gallery
infrastructure. ai-pet-portrait already demonstrates the exact product
shell.
3. A second Etsy/Printify fulfillment universe. Presswork deliberately
relies on Etsy=E2=86=94Printify native fulfillment where possible; we shoul=
d
too.
4. Separate caption positioning logic for preview and export. One
coordinate formula must drive both.
5. Retry-the-whole-order behavior. Every artifact should be durable
and every stage idempotent/resumable.

MOST SURPRISING HOBBY PROJECT
Presswork. It is not visually exciting, but it solves the part most
likely to become painful once Roast.pet gets real orders: autonomous
workers racing, failing, retrying and handing assets to Etsy/Printify
without duplicating products. That is higher leverage than another
generation model.

FASTEST INTEGRATION WIN
Copy Presswork's job-state/locking pattern into a tiny fake-order
harness before touching real Etsy credentials. This can validate the
production architecture without spending money or risking storefront
mutations.

ONE REPO/AUTHOR WORTH FOLLOWING
brac/presswork =E2=80=94 because it is actively working on the exact
autonomous Etsy/POD operational layer Roast.pet will need after the
media pipeline works.

ARCHITECTURE UPDATE
The clean end-to-end system is increasingly:

customer upload
=E2=86=92 async generation job
=E2=86=92 canonical pet package
=E2=86=92 state generation + deterministic QA
=E2=86=92 roast/video/print artifacts
=E2=86=92 immutable design package
=E2=86=92 listing worker
=E2=86=92 Etsy/Printify bridge
=E2=86=92 order/ledger worker

Each arrow is a persisted state transition, not an in-memory chain.
That lets us swap image/video models constantly while the commerce
machinery stays boring and reliable.
